<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lordship of Argos and Nauplia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1212">1212</a>–<a href="/years/1388">1388</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a> was a Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a> created after the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> captured the strongholds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-sgouros">Leo Sgouros</a> in 1212. Held by the de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche">Roche</a> family as part of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and later by the house of Enghien, it passed in 1388 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-of-enghien">Marie of Enghien</a> sold her lands. Nauplia then became a Venetian base.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Early in the 13th century, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, the Greek lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-sgouros">Leo Sgouros</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a> the centre of a domain of his own. After the crusaders under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a> overran central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, his men held out in the citadels of Argos, Nauplia and Corinth, even after Sgouros died in 1208. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a> fell in 1210, then Nauplia, and Argos last. Otto de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche">Roche</a>, Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, played a major part in their capture, and Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-i-of-villehardouin">Geoffrey I of Villehardouin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> rewarded him with the two cities and an income of 400 hyperpyra from Corinth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Otto II de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche">Roche</a> sold the lordship to his brother, Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-i-de-la-roche">Guy I de la Roche</a>, in April 1251 for 15,000 gold hyperpyra and Guy's lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-company">Catalan Company</a> killed Duke Walter I of Brienne at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-halmyros">Battle of Halmyros</a> in 1311 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolid</a> stayed with the Brienne claimants, held for them by the Foucherolles brothers. Walter II of Brienne, mostly absent, failed to recover Athens in 1331-1332 and built new castles at Kiveri and Thermisi before his death at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> in 1356.</p><p>Guy of Enghien, who took the lordship by exchange with his brother Engelbert, settled there, put down a revolt of 1360 against his Medici baillis, became a Venetian citizen on 22 July 1362, and failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in spring 1371. On his death in 1376 his young daughter Maria was married to the Venetian Pietro Cornaro. In 1388, widowed and unable to defend her lands, she sold them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> on 12 December for 700 ducats a year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-palaiologos">Theodore I Palaiologos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nerio-i-acciaioli">Nerio I Acciaioli</a>, aided by an Ottoman army under Evrenos, seized the towns first; Venice soon took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a>, and Theodore ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> on 11 June 1394.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> were never numerous in the district. Local Greek magnates submitted to their new lords but kept their lands, their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> faith and their Byzantine culture, and Byzantine-style churches went on being built. Despite raids by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licario">Licario</a>'s corsairs in the 1270s, the Catalans and the Aydinid Turks under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umur-bey">Umur Bey</a> in 1332, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> was fairly prosperous, with farmland, pasture, vineyards, fisheries and salt flats near Thermisi, and it exported carobs, raisins, resin, acorn dyes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and linen cloth. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1463 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a> in 1540.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lordship of Argos and Nauplia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its garrison held out until about 1210, and Argos and Nauplia until 1212, when they fell to the Principality of Achaea and became the <b>Lordship of Argos and Nauplia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-leo-sgouros">Lordship of Leo Sgouros</a></li></ul></section>
